(* The Basis Library's build file (docs/build.md): its files, in load order,
   each with what it is to a program, by its annotations:

   "always"  the file is compiled before every program
   "demand"  it is compiled before the programs that mention a name it
             provides, and before the files that require it (the default)
   "seal"    it is compiled before the programs that mention a name it
             provides, and for no file of the library: see the end of the list
   "final"   the file is compiled after every program: what is left to do
             when it ends
   "host=swap", "host=no"
             how tests/basis/host/gen-host-basis.sh gives the file to MLton,
             SML/NJ, Poly/ML and MLKit: as it is (the default), without the
             types and exceptions a host must keep its own of, or not at all

   What a file provides -- the structures, signatures, functors and
   top-level types it declares -- and what it requires -- those of other
   files that it names (Deps, lib/compiler/driver/deps.sml; docs/units.md, The graph)
   -- are worked out from the files: `make manifests` writes them to
   lib/basis/MANIFEST (rune --basis-index), which the compiler reads. A name
   the library needs for itself and offers to nobody is written `RuneFoo` or
   `RUNE_FOO`: the documentation leaves those out. A program can still name
   one -- every declaration of a file that loads is in scope -- so the prefix
   is how a reader tells them apart and not a wall. A demand file declares
   modules and types only, so that a program that mentions it loads it (a
   top-level value or fixity directive belongs in an always file); `rune
   --basis-check` checks that. `rune --basis-deps FILE...` prints the files a
   program loads, and `rune --basis all` loads every file. *)
(* The functors that --functors=functions inlines in every unit that
   applies them, not only their own (docs/build.md, M6b of
   docs/plans/functors-units.md): the Basis's own functors, which make its
   structures in units other than theirs -- the words, ints, vectors, arrays
   and slices, the packing, the streams -- and the I/O functors a program may
   apply. Without, a call of Word8.+ or of TextIO's input goes through the
   functor's records. *)
inline functor
  RuneIntNFn RuneWordNFn RuneByteArrayFn RuneStringVectorFn RuneMonoVectorFn RuneMonoVectorSliceFn
  RuneMonoArrayFn RuneMonoArraySliceFn RuneMonoArray2Fn RunePackWordFn RunePackRealFn RunePackReal32Fn
  RunePrimIOFn RuneStreamIOFn RuneImperativeIOFn RunePosixTTYFlagsFn PrimIO StreamIO ImperativeIO
end
annotate "always" "host=swap" in
  initial.sml
end
annotate "always" in
  pervasive.sml
end
